---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Prompting for Research - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell
    chapterId="competitive-research"
    subChapterId="prompting-for-research"
  >
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <p>
        Once you've framed your research questions and gathered your reference
        material, the next step is knowing how to actually prompt the AI to get
        useful competitive intelligence. The difference between a generic
        summary and actionable research often comes down to how you ask.
      </p>

      <p>
        As you work through this process: have the AI
        write its findings to files as you go. Remember the markdown file you
        set up in the previous step with your research questions and goals?
        That's your starting point. As each round of research produces useful
        results, ask the AI to save them alongside it. By the end, you'll have
        a folder of structured research artifacts instead of a long chat thread
        that may be lost, or difficult to wade through.
      </p>

      <h3>Start Broad, Then Drill Down</h3>

      <p>
        Competitive research works best as an iterative conversation, not a
        single prompt. Start with a broader question to get the landscape, then
        follow up with specifics based on what comes back. Trying to get
        everything in one massive prompt usually produces shallow results across
        the board.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
        <Callout variant="success" title="Round 1: Get the landscape">
          <p class="italic">
            "Search the web for the top project management tools that
            specifically target digital agencies with 10-50 employees. List each
            one with a one-sentence summary of their positioning."
          </p>
        </Callout>

        <Callout variant="success" title="Round 2: Drill into specifics">
          <p class="italic">
            "Now search the web and compare the pricing pages of Teamwork,
            Productive, and Scoro specifically for a team of 25 people. What
            does each charge per month, and what's included at each tier?"
          </p>
        </Callout>

        <Callout variant="success" title="Round 3: Get strategic">
          <p class="italic">
            "Based on what you've found, which of these three has the strongest
            positioning around client-facing features like time tracking,
            reporting, and client portals? What gaps do you see?"
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </div>

      <p>
        Each round builds on the last. By the third prompt, the AI has enough
        context from your conversation to give you analysis that's actually
        useful, rather than a surface-level comparison you could find on any
        review site. After a round like this produces solid results, tell the
        AI to save them: "Write what you just found to a file called
        competitor-landscape.md with a markdown table and source URLs." That
        way you capture the work before moving on.
      </p>

      <h3>Feature Comparison Prompts</h3>

      <p>
        Comparing features across competitors is one of the most common research
        tasks, and one where AI genuinely saves time. The key is being specific
        about what features matter to you.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="info" title="Example prompt">
        <p>
          "Search the web for the feature pages of Asana, Monday.com, and
          ClickUp. Compare them across these specific dimensions: 1) Native time
          tracking, 2) Client portal or guest access, 3) Resource allocation
          views, 4) Jira integration capabilities. Format as a comparison
          table."
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <p>
        Asking for a specific format like a table allows you to organize your data. Without that
        instruction, you'll often get long paragraphs that are harder to scan
        and compare. Once the comparison looks good, have the AI save it to a
        file like feature-comparison.md. These individual files become the
        building blocks for your final deliverable.
      </p>

      <h3>Analyzing Public Positioning</h3>

      <p>
        Understanding how competitors position themselves publicly can reveal
        their strategy, target market, and perceived strengths. AI is
        surprisingly good at this kind of analysis when you give it the right
        source material.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="info" title="Example prompt">
        <p>
          "Here's our landing page: [paste URL or text]. Search the web for
          Competitor X's landing page. Compare the messaging: Who are they
          targeting? What pain points do they emphasize? What words and phrases
          keep showing up? Where do they overlap with us, and where are they
          positioning differently?"
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <p>
        This type of side-by-side analysis would take more time to do manually. The
        AI can pull out patterns in messaging, tone, and focus areas in a
        fraction of the time. Just remember to verify its claims against the
        actual pages.
      </p>

      <h3>Pricing Analysis Prompts</h3>

      <p>
        Pricing pages change frequently, which makes them tricky for AI
        analysis. Always explicitly ask the AI to search the web for current
        pricing rather than relying on its training data.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
        <Callout variant="warning" title="Bad prompt">
          <p class="italic">
            "What does Slack charge for their business plan?"
          </p>
        </Callout>

        <Callout variant="success" title="Better prompt">
          <p class="italic">
            "Search the web for Slack's current pricing page. What are their
            current plan tiers, per-user costs, and what's included in each?
            Note the date of the information you find."
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </div>

      <p>
        Asking the AI to note the date of its sources helps you gauge how fresh
        the information is. If it can only find pricing from a year ago, that's
        a signal you need to check the actual page yourself. Save this to a
        pricing-analysis.md file with the dates included so you know when it
        needs refreshing.
      </p>

      <h3>Identifying Market Gaps</h3>

      <p>
        One of the more valuable uses of AI in competitive research is spotting
        gaps in the market. This works best when you've already fed the AI
        information about multiple competitors in the same conversation.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="info" title="Example prompt (after discussing several competitors)">
        <p>
          "Based on everything we've discussed about these five competitors,
          what customer needs or use cases are none of them addressing well?
          Where do you see underserved segments or missing features that could
          represent an opportunity?"
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <p>
        Take the AI's gap analysis as a starting point, not a conclusion. It's
        good at synthesizing patterns across the information you've given it,
        but it can't tell you about customer pain points it hasn't seen data on.
        If you've been saving findings to files along the way, you can also
        ask the AI to read back through all your research files and identify
        gaps across the full set of data, not just what's in the current
        conversation context.
      </p>

      <h3>Tips for Better Research Prompts</h3>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li>Be explicit when you need fresh
          data. Modern tools decide on their own whether to search, but for research
          it's safer to tell them to, so you're never silently working from stale
          training data.
        </li>
        <li>Paste actual URLs or page
          content when possible. AI analysis of real source material is far better
          than AI recollections.
        </li>
        <li>Ask for structured output
          (tables, bullet lists, numbered comparisons). It makes the results easier
          to use in deliverables.
        </li>
        <li>Keep each prompt focused
          on one angle. "Compare pricing" and "compare features" should be separate
          prompts.
        </li>
        <li>When the AI gives you something
          useful, ask follow-up questions to go deeper rather than starting a new
          topic.
        </li>
        <li>Save findings to files after
          each research round. Your chat history will get long and eventually hit
          context limits or might get compressed away. Files persist and can be read back in later sessions.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
        <p>
          When you find a prompting pattern that consistently produces good
          research, turn it into a reusable skill or improve an existing one.
          Skills let you package up your best techniques so the AI applies them
          automatically without you having to remember the right phrasing every
          time. In Structuring Your Output, we'll explore how to build a skill
          that compiles your research files into polished deliverables.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
